Myth: Before dawn had a name, the world crouched inside a cedar’s shadow, and all light slept in a clay jar beneath its roots. A serpent of river-stone guarded the jar, believing darkness kept every creature safe from grief.

But Luma, the fox, wandered nightly through villages where mothers could not see their children’s faces and friends spoke like ghosts across the road. She carried their sighs in her ears until they became a song.

Luma crept under the cedar and sang to the serpent. The song told of bruises hidden, seeds unwatered, and hands searching for hands. The serpent’s granite heart softened. “If I open the jar,” it warned, “the world will see its pain.”

“Then it will also see the path to healing,” said Luma.

The serpent loosened one coil. A silver flame escaped. Luma caught it in her tail and leapt to the highest hill, setting it in the sky as the moon. People looked up and found one another.

Moved, the serpent broke the jar. Sparks streamed upward as stars, while one warm ember rolled east and became sunrise.

Since then, foxes wear pale tail-tips, serpents bask in morning, and light returns because courage once entered the roots.
